# CODEOWNERS -- who must review a change before it can merge.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every ruleset on this repository already sets
# `require_code_owner_review: true`. Without a CODEOWNERS file that rule matches
# no path and therefore requires nothing: the protection reads as enabled in the
# settings UI and enforces nothing on any pull request. A rule that is on and
# inert is worse than one that is off, because it is believed.
#
# TEAMS, NEVER INDIVIDUALS. GitHub does not let an author approve their own pull
# request, so naming one person as the owner of a path makes that person unable
# to land a change to it alone -- the protection turns into a block precisely
# when the owner is the one working. A team can absorb that: another member
# reviews. It also survives the org growing, which a list of usernames does not.
#
# Ownership is deliberately coarse. A per-directory map that nobody maintains
# routes reviews to the wrong people as the tree moves, and a stale owner is a
# review that does not happen.

# Default owner for everything not matched below.
*                                   @znasllc-io/human-developers

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paths where a mistake is not caught by a unit test.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

